<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zoltán Kodály</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kodály Zoltán</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zoltán Kodály was a Hungarian composer, ethnomusicologist and music educator, born in Kecskemét, known for the Kodály method of music teaching. From 1905 he recorded folk songs in remote villages on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phonograph">phonograph</a> cylinders and introduced his friend Béla Bartók to folk-song collecting. His works blend Western European styles with Hungarian folk music; his first major success was the Psalmus Hungaricus in 1923, followed by Háry János (1926) and the Dances of Galánta (1933). From 1935 he worked to reform music teaching in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s schools, and his principles, built on singing and on folk songs in the pupils' mother tongue, spread after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> in 1967.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zoltan-kodaly.jpg" alt="Zoltán Kodály" width="330" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kodály_Zoltán_1930s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1950s the communist leader Mátyás Rákosi planned to replace it with an anthem better suited to communist ideology, but the poet Gyula Illyés and the composer <b>Zoltán Kodály</b>, whom he had in mind for the task, both refused.</blockquote><a href="/songs/himnusz">Himnusz</a></li></ul></section>
